# 17. TECHNICAL REFERENCES AND RESEARCH RULES

Verify the relevant official sources and the status of any standard at implementation time. Do not invent clauses, thresholds, editions, or compliance claims.

Use this research hierarchy:

1. Official standards organisations and their scope/status pages.
2. Authoritative technical handbooks, such as ASHRAE's sound-and-vibration guidance.
3. Recognised scientific or government publications.
4. Traceable manufacturer laboratory test data for specific products.
5. Clearly labelled generic or illustrative reference data only when primary evidence is unavailable.

Useful starting references:

- ISO 3382-2:2008 — measurement of reverberation time in ordinary rooms: https://www.iso.org/standard/36201.html
- ISO 12354-1:2017 — estimation of airborne sound insulation between rooms: https://www.iso.org/standard/70242.html
- ISO 717-1:2020 — airborne sound-insulation ratings: https://committee.iso.org/cms/live/live/en/sites/isoorg/contents/data/standard/07/74/77435.html
- ASHRAE Handbook — Fundamentals, Chapter 8, Sound and Vibration: https://handbook.ashrae.org/Handbooks/F25/IP/F25_Ch08/F25_Ch08_ip.aspx
- WHO Environmental Noise Guidelines for the European Region: https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789289053563

These sources have different purposes. Do not treat a measurement standard as a complete design specification, a rating standard as proof of as-built performance, or outdoor health guidance as an indoor-room pass/fail requirement.

Check the latest publication and revision status before describing a standard as current. If a standard is under revision, clearly distinguish the published edition from a draft or proposed replacement.

Do not reproduce copyrighted standard text. Implement original code and concise, traceable summaries based on permitted source material.

Store source metadata in an auditable way where relevant, including the publisher, title, edition or year, URL or identifier, access/review date, relevant applicability, and any licence constraints.

Do not mark a property “verified” merely because a source name has been added to a record.

# 18. TESTING REQUIREMENTS

Inspect the actual test runner and existing test conventions. Add a dedicated P36 suite and register it using the established mechanism.

Include focused unit tests for:

### Geometry and units
- Valid rectangular-room volume.
- Missing or invalid dimensions.
- Non-finite and negative input rejection.
- Unit conversion, where supported.
- Irregular or unsupported geometry handling.
- Correct surface-area accounting.

### Reverberation calculations
- Known test fixtures with manually verified expected results.
- Correct SI units and formula implementation.
- Frequency-band independence.
- Missing absorption coefficients.
- Zero or invalid total absorption.
- High-absorption or other cases where the simplified model is inappropriate.
- Stable rounding and deterministic results.
- Clear separation between estimated and measured values.

### Evidence integrity
- Unconfigured project.
- User-entered values remaining user-entered.
- Illustrative assumptions not becoming verified evidence.
- Invalid or missing source references.
- Incomplete data producing an appropriate warning or insufficient-evidence result.
- Stale evidence versions invalidating relevant conclusions.

### Acoustic screening
- Each supported assessment domain.
- Missing room adjacency or assembly data.
- Unknown sound-insulation ratings.
- Unsupported STI or advanced simulation requests not producing fabricated results.
- Project-defined criteria versus reference profiles.
- Contradictory criteria and unconfirmed requirements.

### P30–P35 compatibility
- Confirmed P30 constraints remain distinct from assumptions.
- P31 receives only valid, comparable summaries.
- P32 explicit preferences remain distinct from suggestions.
- P33 geometry is not mutated by assessment.
- P34 accessibility-related constraints are preserved.
- P35 material provenance and climate compatibility warnings remain intact.
- Missing optional upstream modules do not crash P36.

### State and persistence
- Assessment survives save and reload.
- New project safe defaults.
- Legacy project compatibility.
- Preview does not mutate the project.
- Successful atomic apply.
- Failed apply restores the prior state.
- Rollback restores the intended supported changes.
- Changed project inputs make prior previews or results stale.
- Fingerprints remain deterministic.
- Unrelated project and upstream data is preserved.

### User interface
- No room selected.
- Unconfigured assessment.
- Incomplete evidence.
- Invalid input.
- Assessment errors.
- Stale result.
- Preview, apply, dismiss, and rollback flows.
- Accessible labels and keyboard interaction where the test environment supports them.

Use realistic fixtures, not tests that simply assert the returned object exists.

For each key calculation, independently derive expected values and test tolerances explicitly.

Do not weaken or delete existing tests to make P36 pass.

